Durarara!! AU
Chapter 16 – Part 2
Curtis finally lowered his gaze back to the phone and typed.
Curtis:
I’ll be here all night.
Turning my ringer on for you.
I’m tired, but I’ll reply if you message.
He sent it, then reached over and switched the ringer on with a quiet click. The apartment settled around the small decision like it mattered more than it should have.
He didn’t add the rest.
Didn’t say that he understood the specific pain of watching someone you loved move too easily around another person. Didn’t say that jealousy was not always loud — sometimes it was just sitting in a room listening to laughter that no longer included you. Didn’t say that he recognized the shape of what she was describing even if he refused to name it without better proof.
That part stayed in his chest.
On the screen, the message remained simple. Available. Uncomplicated.
Across the city, in the dark of her room, Celty would only see that he was still there.
The reply came after a short delay.
Celty:
You don’t have to do that.
A pause.
Celty:
But… thank you.
Another message followed more slowly, like she had to decide whether to send it.
Celty:
Most people say they’re available and then disappear the second it becomes inconvenient.
You keep meaning it.
Celty:
Go ahead and rest if you need to.
I’ll try not to message too much.
Then, quieter still:
Celty:
I’m glad you’re still awake though.
Even if just for a little while.
On the other side of the city, the glow of the PDA lit the dark of her room as she stared at the exchange, helmet set aside, the muffled sound of laughter still faintly present beyond the wall — easier to ignore now that someone had chosen to stay reachable on purpose.
Celty lay on her back in the dark, PDA resting against her chest, and stared at the ceiling she could not properly see.
She knew Mei.
Not well enough to claim her as a true friend of her own, but well enough through Shinra. Close enough to remember the way the two of them had always orbited each other — the easy laughter, the unfinished sentences, the casual contact that lasted a second too long. Even back then it had sat uncomfortably in the room sometimes, like a door left half-open. She had told herself it was just history. Shared study. Old familiarity. Shinra had chosen her. That was supposed to be the end of the equation.
Now Mei was in the other room again. Laughing again. And Celty was in here with a phone in her hands, listening to the sound of a closeness that had never fully died.
Her thoughts turned, almost unwillingly, toward Curtis.
The street.
His voice answering hers without a screen.
The way he had held her without asking her to translate herself first.
The bluntness. The apology. The quiet offer to stay reachable through the night.
If Shinra was allowed that kind of closeness with someone from his past — if that was simply part of what their life could contain — then what exactly stopped her from allowing herself something similar?
Not replacement.
Not a declaration.
Just… space.
The thought unsettled her more than the laughter through the wall.
Because once she asked the question properly, the answer was no longer obvious. And the fact that Curtis’s name arrived so quickly as the other half of it made the dark of the room feel narrower than before.
They had the same thought at almost the same hour.
In his apartment, Curtis set the phone aside and stood. The night had become too crowded with open conversations. He needed speed. Distance. Air that didn’t carry other people’s quiet need. He pulled the sealed helmet from the closet — black and white angular plates, single glowing red visor — then layered the under-armor. White smoke condensed tight around his skin first, sealing every inch of identifiable flesh beneath black coverage before the gray-and-red jacket went on. By the time he rolled the Softail out of the garage, there was no Curtis left to recognize. Only the silhouette the city had started calling the Tech Rider.
Across Ikebukuro, Celty was already in motion.
The laughter through the wall had become unbearable. She set the PDA down, fixed the yellow helmet in place, and called Shooter. The familiar answered at once — not a true motorcycle, never only a machine, but her Cóiste Bodhar wearing the shape of one. Shadow and old Dullahan power disguised as black metal and engine growl. She left the apartment without destination, only the need to outrun the sound of someone else’s ease.

They found each other on a long elevated stretch with almost no traffic.
Celty saw the other rider first: red Softail, sealed black-and-white helmet with a burning circular visor, posture low and controlled. No exposed skin. No face. No readable human identity.
The Tech Rider turned his helmet toward her and gave a single nod.
Then he revved once — clean, deliberate.
Celty’s yellow helmet tilted.
A race?
Hmmm. Why not.
She opened Shooter up.

The night tore open.
They cut hard through empty arteries and service roads, streetlights smearing into streaks. Celty took the first sharp turn with unnatural precision, Shooter leaning further than ordinary suspension should allow. Curtis matched her line on pure control and nerve, the Softail growling under him as he compensated for every advantage her familiar already possessed.
Inside the sealed helmet, his thoughts stayed dry and amused.
Wait a minute. She has no idea it’s me.
Heh. Maybe she can let loose.
She did.
On the next long approach, Shooter left the ground.
It was not a stunt ramp jump. It was an impossible clearance — a gap between elevated sections no stock motorcycle should have attempted, let alone cleared so cleanly. Shadow and familiar power carried the black bike through the air as if the street below were optional.
Curtis had no such luxury.
His Softail was a machine. Expensive, tuned, beloved — but still only a machine.
So he compensated the only way he could.
He kicked free of the seat mid-approach, boots leaving the pegs as he seized the chassis and hurled the entire bike forward across the gap with raw force. For a split second he was airborne behind it, braids sealed and hidden, red visor locked on the tumbling machine. Then he caught it in the air, hauled himself back into position, and forced the landing into something that only just stayed rubber-side down.
The impact slammed through the frame.
Curtis gritted his teeth behind the visor as the Softail shuddered hard under him, engine note rough for a second before it caught again.
“Fuck,” he muttered into the sealed helmet. “That was rough. Maintenance is going to be happening sooner than later.”

Celty had already landed and slowed just enough to look back.
Through the yellow helmet she watched the red bike finish a recovery that should have ended in scraped metal and a body on the asphalt. Instead the rider was still mounted, still moving, still correcting the line like the mid-air dismount and catch had been an ugly but workable plan.
Her real voice stayed inside the helmet, unheard by anyone but herself.
…What?
Shooter idled restlessly beneath her. The other rider was human. The bike was ordinary. And yet he had just treated physics like a suggestion and survived it.
Celty turned forward again, the race no longer only a distraction.
Now it was a question.
They had slowed enough for the engines to drop from a scream into a hard roll. Streetlights strobed across both helmets as Curtis kept pace just off her flank.
His voice came through the sealed helmet’s modulator — filtered, lower, stripped of the cadence she would have recognized from the street and the texts.
“That’s a sweet bike you got there.” A short, rough breath. “Mine couldn’t clear the jump, so I had to compensate.”
Celty’s yellow helmet turned sharply toward him.
For a moment she only stared, Shooter still restless beneath her. The recovery she had just witnessed replayed itself in hard detail: the mid-air abandonment of the seat, the violent throw of an entire motorcycle, the catch, the barely controlled landing. No ordinary rider did that and kept moving. No ordinary bike should have survived it either.
She reached for the PDA mounted near the handlebars and typed one-handed, the screen’s glow reflecting faintly off her visor before she angled it so he could see.
That wasn’t compensation.
That was insane.
A second message followed almost immediately.
Your bike should be in pieces.
You should be in pieces.
She kept the yellow helmet locked on the glowing red visor, waiting to see whether the stranger behind it would laugh, explain, or simply open the throttle again.
Curtis watched the PDA messages for a second, then gave a small shake of the sealed helmet. The voice that came through the modulator was still filtered, still unrecognizable, and faintly amused.
“Hehe. That may be so.”
He lifted two fingers off the handlebar in a brief, lazy farewell.
“See you around.”
The red Softail surged forward before she could type another reply, taillight cutting a clean line down the empty stretch until the night folded around it. Within seconds the Tech Rider was gone, leaving only engine echo and the unsettled quiet of a race that had ended without answers.
Inside the helmet, Curtis’s private thoughts were far less cool.
Oh man. There’s a rattling and it sounds expensive.
That’s on me.
The Softail was still running, but the frame carried the complaint of the jump in every subtle vibration through the pegs and bars. He eased the throttle and took the longer route home, already calculating how much the repair was going to hurt.
Behind him, Celty remained at the edge of the elevated road on Shooter, yellow helmet turned toward the darkness where the other rider had vanished. The PDA screen still glowed with her last unread messages.
Whoever he was, he had treated an impossible gap like a solvable problem and then ridden away laughing.
She did not yet know she had just raced the same man currently saved in her phone.
Celty was still sitting on Shooter at the edge of the empty stretch, yellow helmet turned toward the dark where the Tech Rider had disappeared, when her PDA vibrated.
A private message.
Curtis:
Did you have fun? 🏍️
She stared at it.
For a second the words refused to make sense. She had just raced a sealed stranger with a glowing red visor — a rider who threw his own motorcycle across an impossible gap and then laughed about the damage. That man was gone. And now Curtis, the same Curtis she had been texting from her room, was asking if she had fun, complete with a motorcycle emoji, as if he had been standing on the same stretch of road.
Her gloved hands tightened on the PDA.
Celty:
…What?
A short pause. Then, more carefully:
Celty:
Fun with what.
Celty:
I was out riding.
Why would you ask it like that.
She kept the screen open, yellow helmet angled down toward the glow, the unsettled feeling from the race now tangled with something sharper and harder to place.
On the other side of the city, the Softail rattled its expensive complaint beneath a rider who had not yet decided how much of the night he was willing to leave unexplained.
Celty’s follow-up messages sat unanswered on the screen.
Curtis read them once, the soft glow of the phone reflecting off the inside of the sealed helmet as he rolled the Softail through the quieter streets toward home. The bike still carried the expensive rattle of the jump. The night still carried the shape of a race she did not know they had shared.
He did not type back.
Instead, behind the glowing red visor, he smiled — small, private, and a little guilty at the edges. Not cruel. Just amused by the thin line he was currently balancing on, and by how close the two halves of the night had come to touching without fully colliding.
He locked the phone, slipped it away, and kept riding.
Across the city, Celty remained in the dark with an open conversation and no explanation.
The chapter closed there:
one rider smiling into silence,
one rider waiting for an answer that did not come,
and Ikebukuro holding the space between them like it already knew the truth would not stay hidden forever.
End of Chapter 16.